{
  "dom_id": "step-q107146",
  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "Consider the vector space $C[-1,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[-1,1]\\to R$ and its subset $B=\\{f\\in C[-1,1]\\,|\\,f(1)\\ne f(-1)\\}.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $B$ is closed under function addition\n\nII. $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $B$ is a subspace of $C[-1,1]$\n\nIV. $B$ is **not** a subspace of $C[-1,1]$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if  $f(x)=x$ and $g(x)=-x,$ then $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are in $B,$ but\n\n$$\n(f+g)(x)=x-x=0\n$$\n\n for all $x\\in [0,1].$\nIn particular, \n\n$$\n(f+g)(1)=(f+g)(-1).\n$$\n\n So, $f+g\\notin B.$ Hence, $B$ is *not* closed under function addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. If $f(x)\\in B$ and $k=0,$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(1) & =0\\cdot f(1) \\\\  & =0 \\\\  & =0\\cdot f(-1) \\\\  & =(kf)(-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $kf\\notin B.$ Hence, $B$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $B$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
